  - nested Prop R2, Lemma OD, Prop BT (notes/nested_results.md:332,362,401) and escapeR2 Lemma TI / Cor EX / Prop R3 (notes/escapeR2_results.md:154,183,437): Paper 1 keeps algebraic children (respecting Prop R2), closes each side's algebraic primes inside its own field while certifying squares simultaneously in both K_1 and K_2 (Lemma 5.1, pp. 41-42; §6 Step 4, p. 46 — respecting Lemma TI and Cor EX, since Def 4.5(2) on p. 39 cancels only the shared rational factor a*g_1 - b*g_0 between phi_1(gamma_1(a)) and phi_2(gamma_2(a)), never an algebraic ideal across unrelated fields), and builds trees of total size <= (log_2 N)^{ceil(J/2)+1} <= exp((ln ln N)^2) >> d (Lemma 4.6(1), p. 39, outside Prop R3's kappa*(d - 2) component-size premise in notes/escapeR2_results.md:443-449) whose single paths have r <= 0.65 ln ln N << d ≈ Upsilon/2 nodes (Lemma 2.8(3), p. 13). In notes/nested_results.md:389-390, Lemma OD was explicitly flagged as a method barrier against deterministic box scans (which require joint moment control over > d + 1 tree nodes), not an impossibility theorem. Paper 1 sidesteps both Lemma OD and naive backtracking (Prop BT, notes/nested_results.md:401-413) via proportional Bernoulli(p_acc) acceptance (Def 4.5, p. 38) and backward path telescoping (Lemma 4.6, p. 40, lines 2149-2170): on locally good slices (Z_clean >= mu_t/2), candidate a is selected with probability <= (2/mu_t)*X_a, so conditional expectations backwards along one r-node path at a time cancel mu_t^{-1} (sum_a (2/mu_t)*E[X_a | Y_{<t}] < 3) and union-bound over <= log_2 N children per step ((3 log_2 N)^r * 2 exp(-25(ln ln N)^2) <= exp(-23(ln ln N)^2)), needing polynomial evaluation decoupling at only R <= r + 3 << d points (Lemma 4.2(3), p. 29; compare multidescent Prop UB in BARRIERS.md:49, which used a depth-2 union bound without proportional Bernoulli telescoping).
  - fruit (Prop SR, Prop IV-LV, Thm HYB; notes/fruit_results.md:26,52-55,71,132), rkrank (notes/rkrank_results.md:28-53), and pcest/ldbound (notes/pcest_results.md:25; notes/ldbound_results.md:41-58): Paper 1 does not prove Conjecture F (Lee-Venkatesan Conj. 7.1) for unseeded NFS, nor does it contradict Prop SR or Prop IV-LV. Every row descends from a blinded random square w_m = z_m^2 mod N (§6 Step 3(a), p. 45; Lemma 5.3, p. 44) — pure sealed-root Dixon descent (Theorem DD), where rkrank rank deficits and Prop IV-LV never arise. Fixed-field algebraic pair-correlation obstacles (PC_alg) are resolved by independent two-field discrete Laplace shifts plus ancestor-resultant bad-conductor pruning (Lemmas 3.6(4) and 4.4).

C3. Repurposing toward alpha < 1/3

Nothing in any of the three papers opens a path toward alpha < 1/3:
- Paper 1 stays strictly inside all three walls of our lowerbound model theorem (notes/lowerbound_results.md:42-67,109-120; notes/hatch_results.md:1-105; STATUS.md:382-387):
  1. Hatch H (beyond-Dickman smoothness constructors): The four-fold hybrid weight w_tilde_{y_1,u_0} has main-term density nu ≈ (4 u_0 ln y_1)^{-4 u_0} (Lemma 3.5(3), p. 18), matching ordinary Dickman u^{-u} scaling at u = 4 u_0 (consistent with weighted Theorem J in notes/hatch_results.md:37). Proportional Bernoulli path telescoping eliminates proof-level adaptive-conditioning blowup so rigorous bounds reach the heuristic 1/3 exponent; it does not enrich smooth values above Dickman density.
  2. Hatch LP_exc^rel (sub-profile residual norms): Paper 1 uses standard rank-2 homogeneous degree-d binary forms satisfying N | f_iota(g_0, g_1), for which notes/lowerbound_results.md:66-67 and notes/subthird_results.md:25-45 prove alpha >= 1/3 and c >= 1.5263 (Paper 1 has c = 105).
  3. Hatch R/reuse (sub-factor-base fresh residuals / shared DAGs): Paper 1 collects R_tot = D_row + 3 ≈ L_N[1/3, 18] independent blinded descent trees (§6 Steps 2-3, p. 45) with zero cross-row node sharing or thin-dependency compression (MIN-ROW / THIN-LEDGER in notes/hatch_results.md:88-96 remain intact).
- Paper 2's multiquadratic Walsh-Hadamard / biquadratic cocycle device (Lemma 3.1(3), pp. 13-15; Lemma 5.1, pp. 20-22): This cleanly realizes the V_4 biquadratic splitting-sign F_2-linearity (TIED-PARITY / RM(1, K)) noted in notes/escapeR2_results.md:213-233 and notes/hatch_results.md:97-101. It cannot help alpha < 1/3 for two reasons: (i) constructing compatible modular square roots s_l^2 ≡ d_l (mod N) in Lemma 3.1(3) requires knowing an even multiple M_tilde of lcm_{p|N}(p - 1), which is already factoring-equivalent in L[1/2] (without M_tilde, finding square roots of small d_l mod N is as hard as factoring, notes/escapeR2_results.md:288-296); and (ii) field discriminants D_l <= 4 P_K ≈ K^K grow superexponentially in K, capping K at (ln N)^{1/2} (L[1/2] scale). Part 2's long-progression perturbation box moves upward to L[3/4], away from 1/3.
- Paper 3 (discrete_logarithm.txt, Thm 1.2 p. 2; §1.2 p. 4; Lemma 3.3 p. 12) vs the characteristic obstruction (notes/equivtarget_results.md:31-44): Blocked directly by our proved obstruction that every ring homomorphism Z/NZ -> A into an F_p-algebra with gcd(N, p) = 1 is identically zero (N*phi(1) = p*phi(1) = 0 implies phi(1) = 0). Paper 3's quasi-polynomial descent exp((2+o(1))(ln n)^2 / ln ln ln n) hinges on characteristic-p Frobenius additivity (x + y)^{q_0} = x^{q_0} + y^{q_0} (making root sets of sparse linearized pencils L_Z(T) F_{q_0}-affine spaces and trapping monodromy inside AGL_N(F_{q_0})) and on the ordinary elliptic Frobenius presentation phi_q(I_0) = I_0 + Q. Neither structure exists over Z or number fields (notes/hatch_results.md:41).

C4. Credibility read, constants (105, 40), Lean claim, and referee priorities

- Internal consistency: Mathematically, both factoring .txt papers read as unusually complete, non-circular, and numerically tight — with no missing sections or "Insert statement" stubs of the kind seen in Masullo (notes/masullo_results.md:33-36). Parameters are computed in strict dependency order from explicit rationals (Def 2.7, pp. 11-12 in Paper 1; §8.2, pp. 38-39 in Paper 2), external inputs are standard effective theorems (Prop 2.6, p. 10; Prop 7.1, p. 29), and subtle edge cases that bit our own drafts are explicitly handled: skewness excision on irregular special-q lattices (Lemma 3.6(2), p. 24; cf. notes/nested_results.md:479-482), absorbing primes p | c_{iota,d} into V'_{A_iota} rather than descending them (Lemma 5.1(2), p. 41), clearing index primes via f'_iota(theta_iota)^2 before 3-adic Hensel lifting (Lemma 5.2, p. 43), localization maps avoiding [O_K : Z[theta]] coprimality (Lemma 7.4, p. 34), and semiprime active characters q = p_1 p_2 when all v_2(p_i - 1) are equal (Lemma 3.1(2), p. 13; Lemma 6.3 Type III fiber packing, p. 25).
- Plausibility of constants (105, 40): Both numbers come transparently from conservative proof choices:
  1. Factor-base exponent beta = 18 (B ≈ L_N[1/3, 18]) is set large so bad conductors satisfy f'' >= (1/3) B^{0.99*3/5} >= L_N[1/3, 10.6], beating H_0 * L_N[1/3, 4.01] = L_N[1/3, 4.2 + 4.01] <= L_N[1/3, 8.25] in Lemma 3.6(4) (p. 28, line 1495).
  2. Dense F_2 Gaussian elimination (§6 Step 4, p. 46 — no sparse Wiedemann) on R_tot ≈ 2 d pi(B) ≈ L_N[1/3, 18] rows uses deterministic space O(R_tot^2) <= L_N[1/3, 37.4] < 38 < 40 (p. 47; 38 * 2^{-1/3} ≈ 30.16).
  3. In Step 3(b), M_cap ≈ L_N[1/3, 18 + 3.15] = L_N[1/3, 21.15] root-split trials each recursively call Factor on x_1, y_1 <= sqrt(N)/2. Strong induction (Step 4 of proof, p. 49, lines 2591-2610) requires 21.15 + c * 2^{-1/3} < c, forcing c > 21.15 / (1 - 2^{-1/3}) ≈ 102.52 -> 104 < 105 — our exact (b + 1/b_1)/(1 - 2^{-1/3}) recursion blowup from Theorem NR (notes/nested_results.md:498) and Theorem U-MF (notes/mfU_theorem.md:69).
- Lean 4 claim: Treat with strong skepticism until source code is public. None of the three .txt papers mentions Lean at all; the claim appears only in the GitHub READMEs ("will be released in a companion submission"), with an unexplained version discrepancy — Lean 4 (v4.35.0) in integer_factorization_README.md:24 and discrete_logarithm_README.md:22 vs v4.33.0 in deterministic_factoring_l34_README.md:37 — while claiming zero custom axioms or unproved assumptions, depending only on Mathlib. Mathlib lacks AKS primality, Lagarias-Odlyzko effective Chebotarev, Stark's 1974 Lemma 8, Landau-Page repulsion, Montgomery zero-density, and multitape Turing-machine bit-complexity libraries (compare notes/leanA_results.md:24-28,86-97 and notes/grhremark_results.md:78-82, where formalizing cost-assembly alone took ~6,600 lines with published theorems left as Prop hypotheses).
- Top 3 places a referee should check first:
  1. Paper 1, §4 Lemma 4.6 (pp. 39-40, lines 2123-2173) + Lemma 4.4(1) (pp. 35-37): Check every measurability step in backward path telescoping — charging the computable-vs-analytic filter gap A_alg \ A_clean into node-r failure (line 2130), verifying P_Lap^{x 2} slice conditioning on (f_1(a_s^*), f_2(a_s^*))_{s<t} (including p_1 not dividing f_2(a_0^*) at t = 0, line 2133), and confirming capped rejection/Bernoulli loops (Def 4.5, p. 38) preserve X_a / Z_alg <= (2/mu_t)*X_a after conditioning on non-timeout.
  2. Paper 1, §3 Lemma 3.5(4)(a) & Lemma 3.6(4) (pp. 21-28): Check (g_AP * phi(m'))^{-1} cancellation when absorbing gcd(z_0, m_AP) <= y_1^{3/10} into the unrestricted factor m_1 across smooth cutoff boundaries (lines 1170-1186), and the three-class prime-power partition (lines 1476-1496) ruling out f_flat <= H_0 for bad conductors f in S_bad.
  3. Paper 2, §§5-6 Lemma 5.1 + Lemma 6.3 (pp. 20-26) and §§9-10 Prop 9.3 + Prop 10.1 (pp. 47-55): In Part 1, check semiprime q = p_1 p_2 with small p_1 > B_guard(m), especially Type III 16th-moment fiber bounds (lines 1643-1646) and p_1 | N(a') inclusion-exclusion (lines 1694-1701). In Part 2, check uniqueness and Cauchy-Schwarz in the greedy three-piece split n = m_1 m_2 m_3 (Prop 9.3, p. 48 lines 2924-2949) and stopping-time peeling + Helmert projection preserving H^*_true > 0 (Prop 10.1(2), pp. 54-55).
